Use this appendix to recall a technique, not to select an unfamiliar
write operation. Every pathname is local unless a command explicitly
uses a remote destination. Before any change, ask three questions:
which machine, which directory, and which exact names?

\section{Orient yourself}

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.24in}Y}
\toprule
\textbf{Input} & \textbf{What to look for}\\
\midrule
pwd & The working directory. A path starting with a slash is absolute.\\
ls -la & Directory entries, including names beginning with a dot.\\
ls -ld ./notes & The entry itself, not the contents if it is a directory.\\
cd .. & Move the shell one level up according to its current directory handling.\\
man 1 ls & The local manual for the \texttt{ls} utility; use
\texttt{q} to leave common pagers.\\
command -v sh & What a POSIX-style shell recognizes as \texttt{sh}.\\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\section{Inspect before changing}

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.6in}Y}
\toprule
\textbf{Input} & \textbf{Purpose}\\
\midrule
file ./notes.txt & Ask for an identification of the file type, not a proof.\\
head -n 5 ./notes.txt & Read at most the first five lines.\\
wc -l ./notes.txt & Count newline characters.\\
grep -nF -e ferry ./notes.txt & Show lines containing the literal string and their line numbers.\\
diff -u old.txt new.txt & Display line differences in unified form.\\
tar -tf parcel.tar & List the archive members without extracting them.\\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

For paths with spaces, quote the whole pathname. A leading
\texttt{./} is a practical way to address a local file whose name starts
with a hyphen. A shell pattern such as \cmd|*.txt| is not a regular
expression: the shell can expand it before the invoked command sees
any arguments.

\section{Read the connecting punctuation}

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.42in}Y}
\toprule
\textbf{Form} & \textbf{Effect in a POSIX shell}\\
\midrule
\texttt{a \textbar\ b} & Connect standard output of \texttt{a} to standard input of \texttt{b}.
Standard error is not connected by this operator alone.\\
\texttt{a > out} & Create or, by default, truncate \texttt{out} before running \texttt{a}.\\
\texttt{a >> out} & Create or append to \texttt{out}.\\
\texttt{a 2> errors} & Send standard error (descriptor 2) to a file. Do not put a space
between the 2 and the greater-than sign.\\
\texttt{a \&\& b} & Run \texttt{b} only if \texttt{a} reports success.\\
\texttt{a \textbar\textbar\ b} & Run \texttt{b} only if \texttt{a} reports failure.\\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

The letters \texttt{a} and \texttt{b} stand for commands above, not
literal input. Never direct output back into the file a command must
read. A safe destination has a different name, not just a different
spelling for the same underlying file.

\section{When the next step is unclear}

\begin{checklist}
\item Stop typing if the display contains an unfamiliar continuation
prompt or if the usual prompt has not returned. Identify whether a
program, pager, editor or remote shell is waiting.
\item Read the first diagnostic and check the exact command name,
working directory and operand spelling.
\item Compare what you expected with one observable result. A blank
screen is not reliable evidence of success.
\item Reproduce the issue on a dummy file. Keep originals and compare
an inspected copy before replacing anything.
\item Before acting remotely, verify the destination independently.
Keep sample names, host keys, credential material and personal paths
out of any diagnostic report you share unless required and safe.
\end{checklist}

